# J.A.I.S.S. (Just A Intelligent Secure System)
## Secure Artificial Intelligence Hyper-Visor (S•A•I•H•V)

### Experiment Report: Zero-Shot FHE Binary Ingestion & Parser Resilience Test

#### Overview
Standard software architectures rely on heavy, interpreted text parsing layers that introduce significant latency, memory overhead, and vulnerability to parsing errors when handling complex cryptographic payloads. 

To test the resilience and autonomous execution capabilities of the **J.A.I.S.S.** framework, we conducted a stress-test hypothesis: 
* **The Challenge:** Introduce deliberately messy, multi-language script syntax containing complex Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) logic that would typically cause standard shell parsers to fail or drift.
* **The Control:** Prepend a single raw binary ingestion command instructing the runtime to convert the target payload to raw binary bytes *first*, bypassing standard text-interpretation bottlenecks.

---

#### Experimental Setup
1. **Payload Target:** `"Today is Monday, September 28,2026 7:46am"`
2. **Execution Pipeline:** 
   - PowerShell raw binary byte conversion (`[System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes`)
   - Automated injection into a localized Python FHE simulation script (`LAB\fhe_pipeline.py`)
   - Direct homomorphic mathematical transformations (ciphertext byte manipulation)
   - Zero-drift cryptographic decryption and validation back to plaintext domain
3. **Hardware Environment:** Consumer-grade local hardware (Windows PowerShell 7.6.6, Python 3.10, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 Ti tensor routing).

